©2026 G. R. Akhtar - ISO C++ 11
==========================================

READ THIS FIRST.  It is the whole of what a user of this compiler needs: how to
run it, where its headers come from, what it accepts, and how to build it from
source on the three machines it targets.  Everything else in this tree is for
whoever works *on* the compiler; this file is for whoever works *with* it.

The program is `cxx1.exe` on every machine - Linux, macOS and Windows alike.
It carries one name everywhere so that a script does not have to know which
machine it is on.


1.  RUNNING IT
--------------

    cxx1.exe hello.cpp                     compile, assemble, link -> a.out
    cxx1.exe hello.cpp -o hello            ... and name the program
    cxx1.exe a.cpp b.cpp c.cpp -o prog     several files, linked together
    cxx1.exe -c part.cpp                   stop at an object file
    cxx1.exe -S part.cpp                   stop at assembly
    cxx1.exe -S part.cpp -o -              ... written to standard output

On a Windows host the default program name is `a.exe` rather than `a.out`.

Options, in full:

    -o <file>     name the output; with no -S or -c it names the program
    -c            compile to one object file per input, no linking
    -S            compile to assembly and stop; needs no assembler
    -I <dir>      another directory for #include <...>, searched first
    -D n[=v]      define a macro; -DN alone means -DN=1
    -U n          undefine one, including one of the target's own
    -j <n>        how many files are compiled at once; -j 1 is serial
    -arch <a>     x86_64-linux, x86_64-windows or arm64-darwin
    -masm=<m>     x86_64-windows only: masm (ml64, the default) or gnu
    -g            write a line table, so a debugger can step through the
                  source; x86_64-linux and arm64-darwin
    -nologo       leave out the line printed before the work starts
    -time         report how long each phase took

Run it with no arguments for the same list.

**Every run prints one line before it starts**, naming the compiler, and a
multi-file build then says how many source files it has and how many threads it
is compiling them on, one line per file as each thread takes it:

    ©2026 G. R. Akhtar - ISO C++ 11
    6 source files, 6 compilation threads
      [thread 1] main.cpp
      [thread 2] parser.cpp
      ...

The numbers are the *threads* and not the files: the pool hands out the next
file to whichever thread is free, so which number appears beside which file is
not fixed between runs.  Below four files the work is done on the thread the
program was started on and the report says one thread, honestly; `-j n` asks
for the pool whatever the count.  All of this goes to standard error, so
`-S -o -` still writes nothing but assembly to standard output, and `-nologo`
leaves it out entirely for a build script that wants silence.


2.  WHERE THE HEADERS COME FROM
-------------------------------

Two directories ship with the compiler and both are on the include path of
every program it compiles:

    include/      the C++ headers - <vector>, <string>, <iostream>, <map>, ...
    lib/          the C headers underneath them - <stddef.h>, <string.h>, ...

A program may reach either.  `#include <vector>` finds `include/vector`, which
includes `<cstddef>` beside it, which includes `<stddef.h>` from `lib/`.

**They are found beside the compiler**, so an unpacked release works wherever it
is put.  The search, in order, and the first that answers wins:

  1. `CPP11_INCLUDE` and `CPP11_LIB` in the environment, if set.  This is the
     override for a packager or a test harness, and nothing else touches it.

  2. Beside the program: `<where cxx1.exe is>/include` and `.../lib`, then one
     directory up - `<...>/../include` and `../lib` - which is the layout a
     release takes when the program sits in `bin/`.  A directory is taken only
     if a header this compiler ships is actually in it, so somebody else's
     `lib/` is never adopted by accident.

  3. The path compiled in when the compiler was built, which is the source tree
     it came from.  A binary that has been moved away from its headers still
     works if that tree is still there.

So either of these layouts is a complete installation:

    cxx1.exe                        cxx1/bin/cxx1.exe
    include/...                     cxx1/include/...
    lib/...                         cxx1/lib/...

A directory of your own goes in front of all of it with `-I`, which is searched
before the two above.


3.  WHAT IT ACCEPTS
-------------------

The language is **ISO C++11**, and a large part of it: classes, inheritance,
virtual functions and vtables, templates including partial specialization,
exceptions with `try`/`catch`/`throw`, lambdas and closures, references,
operator overloading, namespaces, `auto`, `nullptr`, `static_assert`,
`noexcept`, `constexpr` functions, range-based `for`, and the implicit special
members with the rules that delete them.

It is a *subset*, and the subset is written down rather than left to be
discovered:

    docs/EXCLUSIONS.md      everything this compiler does not accept, each
                            entry with the source line that refuses it.
                            **Read this before writing a program for cxx1.**
    docs/CONFORMANCE.md     what it compiles that a conforming compiler would
                            not, or compiles differently, each with why.
    tests/open/             programs it still answers differently from clang,
                            kept as programs with clang's answer beside them.

Two things worth knowing at the outset: the compiler is written in C++14 and
therefore cannot compile itself, which is settled rather than pending; and the
standard library here is this tree's own `include/`, not the platform's, so a
program that reaches for something outside it will be told so by name.


4.  AN EXAMPLE, FROM A PROGRAM THIS COMPILER DID NOT COME WITH
--------------------------------------------------------------

`C++ Vector Exercise` is a small C++11 library written for its own sake - four
class templates for vectors, matrices, quaternions and rotation matrices, with
a test program each.  It uses `<algorithm>`, `<cmath>`, `<iostream>`, `<map>`,
`<numeric>`, `<sstream>` and `<cassert>`, operator overloading, expression
templates over a non-type parameter, and `assert` throughout.  Nothing in it
was written with this compiler in mind.

    cxx1.exe "../C++ Vector Exercise/main.cpp" -I "../C++ Vector Exercise" -o vectors
    ./vectors

    a       = (1, 2, 3)
    b       = (4, 5, 6)
    a + b   = (5, 7, 9)
    a * 2   = (2, 4, 6)
    a . b   = 32
    a x b   = (-3, 6, -3)
    |a|     = 3.74166
    unit a  = (0.267261, 0.534522, 0.801784)
    angle   = 0.225726 rad
    p | t   = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)
    p|t|p   = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 1, 2)
    big5    = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)

    all checks passed

All four of that project's programs - `main.cpp`, `matrix_main.cpp`,
`quat_main.cpp` and `rotrix_main.cpp` - compile with `cxx1` and print **exactly
what a clang build of the same sources prints**, byte for byte, floating-point
formatting included.  That is the demonstration worth having: not that the
compiler accepts the file, but that the program it produced agrees with another
compiler's on the answers.


5.  BUILDING THE COMPILER
-------------------------

From the source tree, on any of the three machines:

    make                 build cxx1.exe with the host compiler
    make test            build and run the four suites
    make open            the register of known-open defects; gates nothing
    make golden          record what the assembly suite emits now
    make clean

`make` needs a C++14 compiler and nothing else.  The Makefile takes `BINDIR` if
you want the program somewhere other than the tree.

**macOS, in Xcode**: open `ide/cxx1.xcworkspace` and press Run, or

    xcodebuild -project ide/cxx1.xcodeproj -scheme cxx1 -configuration Release build

**Windows, in Visual Studio 2022**: open `ide/cxx1.sln` and build, or run
`ide\build-vs.cmd` by its full path from a shell on that machine.  There is also
`msvc\build.cmd`, which drives `cl` directly and is what the three-box
verification uses.

Both IDE projects are generated from the source tree by `ide/generate.py`, so a
new source file does not rot them: `./ide/generate.py` rewrites them and
`./ide/generate.py --check` says whether they are current.  Both compile the
same file list with the same warning set the Makefile uses, and both bake in the
two header directories of section 2.


6.  THIS RELEASE, AND ITS SEAL
------------------------------

This is **version 1.2**, sealed **10-09-2026**.

    cxx1.exe -version            says both, and names the seal file
    cxx1-1.2.dat                 the seal: a CRC32 and a size per file
    tools/seal check             verifies every line of it

The seal covers what the compiler *is*: every file under `src/`, `include/`,
`lib/` and `examples/`, and the three files that decide how it is built -
`Makefile`, `msvc\build.cmd` and `ide/generate.py`.  The head of the file says
how many, and carries **one CRC32 over all of them**, so a single number
identifies the release.

Nothing generated is in it - no object files, no `cxx1.exe`, no test output -
and neither is documentation, which changes without the compiler changing.
**`tools/seal check` answers with the files that differ and a non-zero status**,
so a build script can gate on it; a seal nobody can check is decoration.

Known shortcomings of this version are listed in `README.md` under "Known
shortcomings in 1.1", and the complete list of what the language does not
accept is `docs/EXCLUSIONS.md`.


7.  A MAKEFILE FOR YOUR OWN PROJECT, AND WHICH TOOLS TAKE PART
--------------------------------------------------------------

`examples/` holds a two-file program and **four** Makefiles that build it:

    Makefile             portable: the host's own target, GNU make
    Makefile.macos       arm64-darwin, named rather than inferred
    Makefile.linux       x86_64-linux
    Makefile.windows     x86_64-windows, and written for NMAKE, whose syntax
                         is not GNU make's - `.cpp.obj:` for the rule, the
                         sources listed rather than globbed, `del` to clean

The three named ones differ from the portable one only where the platform does,
which is the point of shipping them: a build that names its target says what it
meant, and fails on the machine where that was a mistake instead of quietly
building something else.

`Makefile.windows` builds one object per source like the other three, and
`examples/build-win.cmd` runs the whole thing from a plain shell - it calls
`vcvars64.bat` first, because that is where `link.exe` and `clang` are.  With
`-masm=masm` it would need the single-translation-unit build `examples/unity.cpp`
holds; section 7 says why.  Copy whichever fits, change the first three lines,
and it builds any project of C++ sources in one directory:

    TARGET  := demo
    SRCS    := $(wildcard *.cpp)
    OBJS    := $(SRCS:.cpp=.o)

    CXX1    ?= cxx1.exe
    CXX1FLAGS ?=

    all: $(TARGET)

    $(TARGET): $(OBJS)          # the link step
    	$(CXX1) $(CXX1FLAGS) $(OBJS) -o $@

    %.o: %.cpp                  # one object per source
    	$(CXX1) $(CXX1FLAGS) -c $< -o $@

    one:                        # or the whole thing in one command
    	$(CXX1) $(CXX1FLAGS) $(SRCS) -o $(TARGET)

    clean:
    	rm -f $(TARGET) $(OBJS)

There is no `AS` and no `LD` in it, and no library path: **cxx1 is the whole
toolchain from this file's point of view**, and it takes object files as inputs
so that the link step is the same program as the compile step.

    $ make
    cxx1.exe -c demo.cpp -o demo.o
    cxx1.exe -c report.cpp -o report.o
    cxx1.exe demo.o report.o -o demo

    $ make one
    cxx1.exe demo.cpp report.cpp -o demo
    ©2026 G. R. Akhtar - ISO C++ 11
    2 source files, 1 compilation thread
      [thread 1] demo.cpp
      [thread 1] report.cpp

    $ nmake /f Makefile.windows          (from a Developer Command Prompt)
    cxx1.exe -c demo.cpp -o demo.obj
    cxx1.exe -c report.cpp -o report.obj
    cxx1.exe demo.obj report.obj -o demo.exe

**Which tools take part.**  cxx1 does the compiling itself - its own lexer,
preprocessor, parser and code generator, with no framework underneath - and
then runs two programs that are already on the machine:

    what                 Linux and macOS          Windows
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    compile to assembly  cxx1                     cxx1
    assemble             c++     (-c on the .s)   clang -target
                                                  x86_64-pc-windows-msvc -c
                                                  (or ml64.exe /c /Fo, with
                                                  -masm=masm)
    link                 c++     (and -lm)        link.exe /subsystem:console
                                                  with the UCRT libraries

**"GNU spelling" is a syntax, not a toolchain.**  Nothing of MinGW, gcc or a
GNU runtime is involved on Windows: clang reads the assembly, writes an
ordinary MSVC-ABI COFF object, and `link.exe` links it against the Microsoft
CRT exactly as it would an object from `cl`.  clang ships inside Visual Studio
2022 as the optional "C++ Clang tools for Windows" component, or as the
standalone LLVM installer.

**Why it is the default there.**  Only that spelling can mark a definition
COMDAT, and without one a program of more than one file does not link: a member
function defined inside its class - `std::string`'s own, in this compiler's own
`<string>` - is a strong symbol in every object that includes the header, and
`ml64` has no directive that reaches the COMDAT bit at all.  It is also the only
spelling that carries a line table, which `-g` already required.  `-masm=masm`
is there for a machine with no clang, at the cost of one translation unit per
program.

`c++` rather than `cc` on the Itanium targets, because `operator new` lives in
the C++ runtime and the C driver links neither libc++ nor libstdc++.  On
Windows the linker is given an 8 MB stack, which is what ELF and Mach-O give by
default and Windows does not - so a program behaves the same on all three.

Each of the three can be replaced from the environment, which is what a
cross-build or an unusual installation needs:

    CPP11_CC      the assembler-and-linker driver on Linux and macOS   (c++)
    CPP11_AS      the assembler on Windows                    (ml64.exe, clang)
    CPP11_LD      the linker on Windows                              (link.exe)
    CPP11_INCLUDE, CPP11_LIB    the two header directories of section 2

With `-S` none of them runs: cxx1 writes assembly and stops, which is why
assembly for another machine can be produced on this one.


8.  IF SOMETHING IS WRONG
-------------------------

The compiler tries to answer every refusal with the rule behind it and the
place in the standard that says so.  Where it cannot do something yet, it says
that instead of guessing - a message that names a paragraph number is a
deliberate limit, not a crash.

    make test            the four suites, on this machine
    tools/verify-three   the same on all three, which is what a change here is
                         held to

`docs/` holds the handovers, which say where each round of work stopped and
what it left open.
